Reinhard Vock <reinhard.vock@lottery.co.at> wrote in message
news:383A717B.AC846308@lottery.co.at...
> Hi
>
> Due to a HD Problem I had to install Suse 6.1 without any swapspace; now
> I got my HD back, labeled partitions etc but now I can not tell Suse to
> access the swap partition; I know I am just not seeing it,
>
> please make me clearvoyant,
>
> Reinhard

Enter a line in /etc/fstab, specifying where to find the swap partition(s).
It looks as follows:

/dev/<disk>  swap  swap  defaults  0  0

where <disk> is the partition you designated as swap partition. If
necessary,
run mkswap on this partition to format it as swap space (probably not
necessary, since you already used it as such). Also, make sure that
somewhere in your boot scripts is a command /sbin/swapon -a, to
install the swapspace at boot time. Then enter swapon -a from the
shell prompt to enable the swap space, or reboot.

Martin Sinot
martin@spase.nl






