Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha,comp.sys.dec


First, benchmarks are easily misused.  For this to have much
relevance you would have to meet the following requirements:
	Run codes very similiar to the spec95 codes.
	Run the operating systems AND compilers used in those benchmarks.
	Analyze price/performance and similiar for your budget/requirements.

Personally I've found that the 21264's + Linux + Compaqs Fortran/C
compilers to perform quite well compared to the Intels'.  Compilers
can easily make a factor of 2 difference on both platforms so
make sure your comparing the right ones.  Most notably there can
be large performance differences between egcs and intel's carefully
tuned compilers.

> Seriously, I need to decide what to choose pretty quickly.  UP2000 with 
> two alpha CPU's at 650 MHz or or a Beowolf cluster with either Intel 
> or AMD.  I have avoided Beowolf because I thought programming for MPU
> is something beyond me.   I like vector CPUs.  But the days are gone.

Well depends on your application, compilers, os, programming skills,
theres no short, easy, correct answer based on the information
provided.  Things like communications costs, algorithms used and
similiar will have large effects on how parallel codes perform.

> Should I really learn about Beowolf?

If you want the maximim price/performance, have algorithms/codes
that are amenable to parallelization, and are willing to learn
the skills/time required then yes.  If not get the fastest single 
cpu for YOUR code you can afford.

I've seen many beowulf/clusters mostly idle with just single
cpu jobs running occasionally.

> Thank you.

> G. Hugh Song



