From: j.l.gomez-dans@sheffield.ac.uk
Subject: " Alpha Buying tips"
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha
Organization: 
Summary: 
Keywords: 

[I don't know whether the initial message got through. If it has, excuse the
[re-post.
Hi!
	We are considering buying a new computer for number crunching
applications. So far, we have been using an aging P200 with Debian 2.1/2.2
on it, but it's taking several hours to process some data and there's some
money available X-). In principle, I have short listed two candidates for the
new "beast": an Athlon based system or an Alpha based  system.

	The use of these machines would be running custom programs in
Fortran (using g77 as a compiler, even though I'm aware that Compaq will
release/has released their compilers  to work with Linux) or C (gcc),
running mathematical software such as scilab, octave, MuPAD. Also, 
I'd like to use slatec/lapack/what_have_you and pgplot. Some discretionary
use of Numerical Python and latex/lyx/klyx. Apart from those power uses,
I tend to use e-mail and to surf the web, sprinkled with "liberal amounts" 
of MP3ing X-))). As far as I can tell, the Alpha workstation would be a 
very capable workhorse for all the above. The Athlon based system would 
probably do all the above quite happily as well.

	The only thing that I'm a bit weary of is  the fact that when  I'm 
done here, someone will want to use this computer. This means that unless 
things have changed a lot in a couple of years and people dump MS Office among
other things, chances are the hard drive will be swiped and someone will try
to install Windows NT, 2000 or whatever they come up with by then. I heard
rumours of MS dropping alpha support (even though Win200 technical
documentation seems to have a whole appendix on installing this on an Alpha
based system). Of course, the problem lies also in whether the would-be user
would want to use Intel oriented-software. So that's what I'm not too sure
about.

	And then, of course, there is the money business. So far, I have
found it very hard to get quotations of Alpha prices. Everyone I have asked
so far has either said "What?" or "We need to do some research on that". So,
does anyone have a clue of how what system would be *nice* to have (meaning
motherboard+alpha microprocessor combination, memory estimates), and how the
price of this would compare to a similar Athlon? From some of the vendors
I've seen posted in this ng (based in Germany, can't remember the name), the
alpha prices tended to be greater than the Athlon (similar hard drive,
graphics card and all that. I think I recall memory being different).

	At any rate, even though I have a feeling the alpha'd be great, some
advocacy needs to be done to convince people that it's the thing  to go for.
This is hard, as even an AMD chip is regarded as non-intel, and therefore,
as "weird". 

	I hope you can excuse my newby-ness when it comes to Alphas and
Linux. So far, the usual paths to find information didn't leave anywhere. I
checked the web pages, but they seem more focused on the happy owners of
Alphas. If this is a FAQ (I get the feeling it is :D), I apologise.

	Thank you for your time,
	Jose

-- 
Jose L Gomez Dans		PhD student
				Sheffield Centre for
				Earth Observation Sciences (SCEOS)
				University of Sheffield UK



