[...]

>         The use of these machines would be running custom programs in
> Fortran (using g77 as a compiler, even though I'm aware that Compaq will
> release/has released their compilers  to work with Linux) or C (gcc),

If you decide for an x86 system (Athlon) you should use gcc 2.95. g77 included in
gcc 2.95 is up to 30% faster than egcs delivered with most distributions and
according to our tests among the fastest Fortran compilers you can get (unless
you do not need Fortran 90 or 95).

g77 on an Alpha system (at least the egcs version, I do not know about gcc 2.95)
is terribly slow. The code compiled with the now available Compaq Fortran
compiler is about twice as fast. I'm sure you will be disappointed about the
performance of the Alpha system when you use g77 (at least we were until we
switched to the Compaq Fortran compiler).

[...]

>         The only thing that I'm a bit weary of is  the fact that when  I'm
> done here, someone will want to use this computer. This means that unless
> things have changed a lot in a couple of years and people dump MS Office among
> other things, chances are the hard drive will be swiped and someone will try
> to install Windows NT, 2000 or whatever they come up with by then. I heard
> rumours of MS dropping alpha support (even though Win200 technical
> documentation seems to have a whole appendix on installing this on an Alpha
> based system). Of course, the problem lies also in whether the would-be user
> would want to use Intel oriented-software. So that's what I'm not too sure
> about.

It seems as if MS and Compaq are dropping Win NT on Alpha (I heard rumours that
NT 4 will still be supported, but Win 2000 will not be ported to Alpha). I also
heard that things might change again when 64 bit NT will be released - but that
won't be before Intels Merced is finished. But don't quote me on this, it's the
same rumours you heard...


Alex Mattausch




