Here's a short story on my Alpha now that it seems to work =)

I got it second-hand quite a while ago. It was an axppci33 mobo with
21066@166MHz, 32M and 810M SCSI drive (Trailblazer). It had RH4.2 on
it. It worked fine for a long time until it started giving weird errors on
compiling etc. I suspected the ram and got another fresh 2x16M (damn
expensive here and then) but it was no different. So I put all the 4x16M
in and installed RH5.2 one christmas. I lived with the machine happily and
recently when I got to a solid ether netlink, I installed RH6.0.
Then the trouble started.
X started hanging the machine. I removed xfs and changed modes and did all
I could, read and read and upgraded and it still doesn't work. My card
might be dead. I upgraded the kernel frequently and more programs
broke. So far only 2.2.9 seems to work fine.
Nowadays I have a HP7550 CD-RW in it and it's in a special case with amp
and speakers inside to cut down on the wiring and desk space. I'm now
moving again and it's coming with me. 
The case now sits atop a Sparc 1+ I bought some time ago as an xterm (I
ran Linux on it, but that sucked, I ran NetBSD happily, then OpenBSD even
happier and then Solaris 7). The original 810M scsi disk is still inside
as well as a cheap 2.5G Bigfoot and the CD-RW. The internal amp and
speakers are driven by a GUS Extreme card with a busted GF1 chip and
there's an Interwave (GUS PnP) card with 5M (?) RAM. The pci buses are
filled by a DS64 (S3), DEC Tulip and 3c590.

All works fairly well. Only X is dead and I'm totally clueless on how to
load patches or do anything with the Interwave synth. I had a sbawe32 for
a while and with pmidi, playmidi and sfxload, all was well.

Softwarewise I have RH6.0 with a lot of rawhide updates (including newer
libc), 2.2.9 and ALSA. I also use CCC to compile anything and everything I
manage. It's not the fastest of machines, but it works very well and is
very dear to me and my wife. I have only good to say about Linux on Alpha
and Alpha and Linux =)   (only some x86 and sparc gripes but that's
another story).

-- 
Harri Haataja




