Opie wrote:
> 
> Ok, here goes,
> 
> I am trying to install Debian on an alpha mulita 166 using the arc
> menus.  I am trying to boot from a floppy.  When it starts to load
> milo from the floppy it freezes at:
> 
> "Swapping to PALcode at 0x0 ..."


I had that a couple of times, in the end it turned out to be... bad
floppies!
Only the fifth one worked at one install session... the kind of thing
that drives you nuts.  So I try to avoid using /dev/fd altoghether.

Only the very first boot you'll have to use the floppy, (at least: I
never got ARC to boot from an IDE CDROM), 

after the debian install boots, it can work over NFS,from a CD (both ide
and scsi), or, if you have no network and no cdrom, from a partition
already on harddisk to install the kernel and the base system.  What I
often do is to put the harddisk that debian has to be installed on
temporarily in an already working system, partition it, and put
resc1440.bin, driv1440.bin and baseX_X.tgz on the partition that will
eventualy become /home 

then in the debian installation, don't partition and format, just mount
the already formatted partitions, except the /home one, and use that
latter to install kernel and base system from.

With this 'transplantation' scheme, it should also be possible to put a
bootable milo+kernel on the new harddisk, but this I haven't tried. 

Hope this was of any help.

Luuk



