Don't know about guides, but do read the Alpha related howtos etc and
especially the stuff on the alphalinux page.
Mostly it's a matter of selecting the motherboard you want to use, then
get a matching case within your budget for it et al. Take care to find out
wheather the board need FPM (_true_ parity) RAM (I don't know if they all
do) and make sure you get some and make sure you get enough (I believe 32M
will run out fast, 64 will do fine and the more the merrier). Get a large
hdd, either IDE or SCSI since you're running Linux, both are ok
(AFAIK.. srm?). Assembling them should be a lot easier than intels. Learn
your bootloader and if you can select between ISA and pci, pick pci and
you won't have to do configuring.

> Where can I get a boot floppy from to partition the drive etc, or would it
> just be a matter of booting straight from the Linux CD & running fdisk? I'll
> be using a Alpha PC164 motherboard with 500 mhz 21164 cpu.

RH bootimages are on the CD or ftp mirror.
Maybe the CD boots? Don't know, I've always done installs over the Net.

> Finally, is there any problem with using large (IDE) hard drives on this
> motherboard? I know several older PC motherboards have problems with disks
> over 2.1mb etc. If so can this be fixed with a new bios or 'arc'?
       ^^^^^  That's a mighty small drive  ;)

No. AFAIK not. Ever.
Selecting an overkill drive will slow down boot and especially fsck, BTW.

I had used Amigas and other ppls' PC's, Macs, C=64's etc before I got the
Alpha and it was my first "big" computer and it was easy (though I didn't
install from scratch the first time). I still have this old axppci33 (I'm
typing on it =)  and it works just great. One gotcha: X doesn't work on
RH6 anymore no matter what. My DS64 might have finally broken.

I also recommend getting CCC and trying to use it for all your compiling
(C) needs. Check also compaq's page for the testdrive if you want to see
Linux run on big alphas (over telnet it won't do much, but you'll see it
run).

And there's people to help on #linux and here.

