> No. AFAIK not. Ever.
> Selecting an overkill drive will slow down boot and especially fsck, BTW.

I have a 13GB IDE-disk in my Alpha box. The AlphaBIOS only reported back
8 GB. Moreover, after partitioning the disk, and thus having logical volumes
past the 8 GB-border, I discovered that, although the disk still could be
booted
and functioned properly in Linux, it was no longer possible to change the
AlphaBIOS boot entries. The AlphaBIOS reported that the drive was offline,
i.e. it didn't exist anymore. I now have an 8 GB disk to boot from, which
doesn't show this problem, and use the larger disk as secondary disk.
My conclusion: Large IDE disks (>8GB) function properly in Linux (you can
access the entire disk without problem), but can give problems with the
AlphaBIOS, although booting is possible from partitions within the first
8 GB.

