Organization: National University of Singapore
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=iso-8859-1
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha

Both the 164 and the 264 have 2 fp units, the difference being that the
264 supports OOO while the 164 does not. So in theory, at X MHz, you can
expect 2X Mflops PEAK on the 164 and 2X Mflops SUSTAINED on the 264.
Conversely, if you can get both your 164's fp units working, you can also
expect to see 2X Mflops. (Why, I know of a guy who's getting almost
1.2Gflops sustained doing matrix computations on his LX 600MHz). 

I am very rusty with FFTs (touch and go affair during my undergraduate
days eons ago) but IIRC, it is basically MAC type of operations. So if
your program is well-optimised, you will have have one fp unit doing the
multiplies and the other doing the adds - in-order execution of your code
with both fp units busy. The 264's OOO support serves little or no purpose
here. Hence, all else being equal, clock speed will be the deciding
factor. 

Stephen Wong
Department of Computational Science
National University of Singapore

