In article <Pine.GSO.3.95.991108212115.6710A-100000@holyrood.ed.ac.uk>,
Peter Boyle <pboyle@holyrood.ed.ac.uk> writes:

>has anyone managed to get assembly source on a linux alpha that
>contains wh64 (new to ev6) compiled on a linux box. I have
>compaq fort/ccc but they seem to use gnu as to compile .s files,
>which doesn't recognise the opcode.
>
>This is a real shame since one piddly wee instruction
>it makes an approx 130MB/s difference
>on my simulation code (620->750) and a 100Mflop increase
>in the performance on a ds10. 
>
>Anyone got any ideas???

Be careful when you locate a version of the assembler that accepts the
wh64 opcode.  The first implementations of wh64 in the GNU assembler
for Alpha did not properly encode the unused register fields for this
instruction.  An incorrectly encoded wh64 can hurt performance rather
than help it.

Normally a Compaq compiler will directly produce a .o object file
without using a .s assembly file as an intermediate.  Under these
normal conditions the wh64 instruction will be properly encoded.




