No.  The commands in Redhat won't be any different than Caldera, or for 
that matter, any other Linux distribution.  They all use BASH, which is 
your shell (command prompt).  Thus, the keywords from your command prompt  
are linked to /bin or /usr/bin or whatever.  As for the text editor, use 
(and learn how to use) VI.  Call it from bash simply with vi filename.  If 
you don't wish to learn VI (it's kind of strange at first), try using 
emacs.  Either way, you will need to know how to use one of these editors.  
The filename that you want to modify, if I understand you correctly, is the 
runlevel for the boot.  This is controlled by a line of code somewhere in 
your boot files, which are located in /etc/rc.d.  If you cd to /etc/rc.d, 
you will find several files, and since the name of the specific file 
escapes me at the moment, you will have to hunt around for it (I think its 
/etc/rc.d/rc.init or something.)  You will need to be root to modify any 
of these files.
 
