<david_morse@my-deja.com> wrote in message
news:80l2s1$cfs$1@nnrp1.deja.com...
> Hi all,
>   Is there any way in which "addq X, literal, Y" is superior to "lda
> Y,literal(X)"?  That I can tell addq only supports an 8 bit literal,
> while lda supports 16 bit literals.  Why would I ever want to use the
> addq version?
>
> PS: Thanks Jon, Mark, and Peter for your responses on assembly
> documentation.  Those docs have helped quite a bit.
>
>
> Sent via Deja.com http://www.deja.com/
> Before you buy.

As far as I can see, the only reason you would want to use addq is
if you want to be able to generate an exception. Other than that,
I see no difference in performance between the two. And then lda
has the advantage of being able to add a signed 16-bit integer,
while addq can only add an unsigned 8-bit integer. You might
still want to use addq for readability reasons, though.

Martin Sinot
