I am looking for a distribution with NIS+ setup preinstalled.
Within /usr/doc/HOWTO/NIS-HOWTO..., I can find:


  The Linux NIS+ client code was developed for the GNU C library 2.
  There is also a port for Linux libc5, since most commercial
  Applications are linked against this library, and you cannot recompile
  them for using glibc. There are problems with libc5 and NIS+: static
  programs cannot be linked with it, and programs compiled with this
  library will not work with other libc5 versions.

  You need to retrieve and compile the GNU C Library 2.1 for Intel based
  platforms, or GNU C Library 2.1.1 for 64bit platforms.  As base System
  you need a glibc based Distribution like Debian 2.x, RedHat 5.x or
  SuSE Linux 6.x.

  For every distribution, you need to recompile the gcc/g++ compiler,
  libstdc++ and ncures. For Redhat, you need to make a lot of changes of
  the PAM configuration. For SuSE Linux 6.0, you need to recompile the
  shadow package.

According to the above information, no distribution is actually ready
for 
NIS+.  However, it appears that SuSE is better prepared for NIS/NIS+. 
The 
official maintaniner of NIS+ is with SuSE anyway.  I am getting sick
with 
the RedHat configuration for Linux/Alpha.  Is it about time to switch 
to SuSE?

Let me tell you why I got interested in NIS/NIS+ in the first place.
A combination of NFS/automounter checks UID/GID only.  If the root
passwd
of the client side is managed out of the central control, the root of 
the client can actually access to the server by setting up a user
account

with the known UID/GID.

Can Secure.rpc or Kerberos deal with this security hole without the 
setup of NIS/NIS+?

Thanks in advance.


