Folks,

This is my first foray into installing Redhat 6 onto anything other than Intel 
clones, and a few problems have kept me scratching my head over the last few 
days:

(BTW, I'm trying to install Redhat 6 onto a Digital XL266 w/64MB ram and have 
installed the latest firmware update) 

I've read the various FAQ's and Howto's on MILO and Redhat 6 and now have a CD 
burnt from an ISO, and three floppies created from image files using rawrite: 
One with MILO for the XL architecture, a generic alpha kernel, and a ramdisk 
floppy.

I can get past the ARC screens into MILO without a problem.

However, when I try to boot from fd0 I get some disk errors reporting that the 
data can't be read. Using other generic disks also using rawrite (from a PC 
running NT WS) doesn't help at all. Trying to ls fd0: also gives the same 
error, 
even using different floppies burnt with the same information.

OK, so trying a different approach I've tried to boot directly from the CD I 
creased from an ISO. I get a bit further and you can see code loading, but end 
up with the message:
Warning: unable to open an initial console.

I'm a bit stumped now - both the floppy and CD installs have failed :-/

Are there any tips on generating the floppies I could try (e.g. don't use a NT 
WS?) or recommendations for programmes to burn a CD from the ISO of the Alpha 
Redhat 6 CD?

I'm keen to see how much faster this setup is running Redhat 6 than when it 
used to run NT4 for Alpha..

Cheers,

        Ben.




