From: Andy <andy@microsoft.demon.co.uk>
Subject: Admit it folks, Linux is a waste of HD space
Date: 1999/11/13
Message-ID: <s2r85a7lhsq32@news.supernews.com>
X-Deja-AN: 548177367
Content-Type: text/plain
X-Complaints-To: newsabuse@supernews.com
Organization: Posted via Supernews, http://www.supernews.com
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha
Content-Disposition: inline

I have been messing about with RedHat 6 for about a month now, and I have 
came to the conclusion that this OS (although it is mostly free) sux 
donkeys!  There is no (as yet) USB support, Inkjet printer support is very 
poor, mouse support is limited, as is keyboard support (no support for 
extra web keys etc.., always behind in Graphic card support, Modem support 
is virtually non existent, Joysticks and joypads forget it, scanners 
forget them too, digital camera support is only good if you bought one 
about 5000 years ago, all the up to date cameras have no support, it gives 
you a major headache when trying to configure any unlisted hardware, for 
god sake even a simple external modem gives people trouble.  There are 
mice which don't work, certain mobo chipsets make linux freak out, why 
does anyone even try???  As for the crap software you get, well GIMP is 
crap, no-where near as good as Photoshop or paint shop pro, despite what 
you Linuxers think, it sux, face it!  Star Office???  Should be called 
Crap bugged to f**k imitation but not a patch on MSOffice.  Remember an OS 
is only good if it gets software support, and I don't really think one 
buggy Office suite and a crap graphics package cuts it really.  

As for games, well, what can I say, the eighties were crap, why do you 
linuxers want to hark back to it, with sub standard tetris clones, pac man 
clones, chess games with crap AI and terrible graphics, mahjong tile 
games, very bad arkanoid clones etc. etc. Whereas on Win98 we can enjoy 
Homeworld, Half Life, Quake 1,2 and 3 (running properly and slightly 
quicker than on linux) and Sys Shock2.  So, so much for open source (all 
these great programmers writing leading edge stuff for Linux)  Yeah, 
where, believe me I've hunted around all over the web and I've found 
nothing worth bothering with, this so called leading edge software that 
the Linux peeps have developed are all poor shadows of already well 
established windows packages/utilities/games etc.  I challenge you.. name 
one thing on linux which is better than Win. .. anything..  Don't any of 
you dare say that any of the GUI's are an improvement on explorer coz it 
isn't true, KDE sux, GNOME sux, even making a simple desktop shortcut is a 
pain in the arse.  

Installing software can be a nightmare, everything remains vague, you rpm -
ivh something and whoosh... welcome to bloatware... where did all that lot 
go you think to yourself. mmm. no friendly screens telling you where its 
gone to, but you are safe in the knowlege that about 36 million files have 
just spewed all over your HD somewhere, then you run the program.. whirr, 
whirr, whirr, nothing.. .nothing.. whirr whirr, still nothing. whirr 
whirr. grinding halt.. mmm you think .. what was that???  and you never 
really find out.. 

Oh and there was another rumour which I have now finally laid to rest as a 
lie, Quake (everyone said it ran much better on Linux) oh really???  It 
took an age to set it all up, and it runs like a dog, well not really like 
a dog, but about 5fps slower than in Win98, and where the f**k is the Half 
Life patch (prob coz all Half Life player realise the same as me that 
Linux isn't worth the headache).

There's a new frikkin kernel released about 6 times a day. Okay, I must 
admit that there is no longer the blue screen of death, but then again if 
all I had on my win98 drives were a crappy freeware graphics package, a 
slightly bugged and very limited freeware (there are plenty of them for 
win98) office suite, and the now bloated Netscape, along with a few paltry 
windows 3.1 desktop games and my tcp/ip net connection, I bet win98 would 
remain stable.  

The reasons for win98 crashing are varied and many, but it must be said 
that most computer owners who do not install demos, utils, betas etc.. 
have very stable systems.  Linux doesn't have many new things released 
that anyone would bother with, you spend about 8 years just getting all 
your hardware up and running and downloading kernels and more hardware 
patches and tinkering with code to try and force the damned operating 
system to recognise that new scanner, and after about a decade and 30gig 
of HD space you are proudly running an OS, with about 3 software packages 
on it.  Windows always gets accused of being bloatware, but now perhaps 
you understand why it is so large, remember that around 95% of hardware 
will be recognised and installed and (a miracle amongst you lot) work 
first time with win98se.  I know Linux is free, but you could always buy a 
pirate copy of Win98 instead, I doubt it if Bill G would really mind, if 
you are a bit hard up.  Anyway my soundcard patch has nearly downloaded 
(28meg of it), so I shall go.  I might go back to Redhat in about a year, 
then again win2000 will be out and the linux community will still be 
shouting about how one day they might catch up..  

So long.. suckers..

