daha@best.com (David S. Harrison) writes:

> Are there any operating system limitations on the amount of RAM
> that can be addressed by Linux when running on the Alpha?

Yes. In the 2.2 kernel, PCI driver limitations restrict you to 2 GByte
for a single system. I think that 2.4 is going to have a proper way to
rewrite drivers so that this limit is removed.

The kernel in general is 64-bit clean.

> My (very
> preliminary) searches have found that few Alpha boards are
> capable of addressing more than 2Gb let alone 4Gb.

It is my impression that Compaq's motherboard literature is incorrect
-- PC164LX boards supposedly can only handle up to 512 megs, yet I
have more than that in one. I suspect that the modern Tsunami chipset
works correctly with 512 MB dimms, and so your typical 8-dimm
motherboard can actually have 4 gigabytes. But you'd have to try it to
be sure; I have only experimented with a PC164LX so far.

-- greg



