: Yes, that's my point, too. However, I'd start by picking some easier
: examples... Most likely, you're a better programmer than me, but
: having failed on XEmacs myself (even with gcc, it's tricky), I find
: porting libc to another compiler to be rather adventurous.

: While speaking of XEmacs: is it normal that it runs twice as slow
: on my SX164 than it ran on my old AMD-K6-166?

I'm not familiar with the innards of Emacs or XEmacs, so what I say is
likely to be completely bogus. But a few things to look for might be
byte-oriented memory accesses or manipulations; I remember reading that
on some Alpha implementations byte-sized access has to be emulated in
PALcode and therefore can be quite slow, whereas on older systems that
were designed with byte-sized operations in mind (such as x86) the cost
is not so high. However, I thought the 21164PC (which the SX uses) had
byte-operation extensions built in...

Ryan

