> So far, it seems that there are two little trays in the front. What are
> these for? Micro CD's? 2.5" harddrives (like a laptop)?

I've got a floppy in one.  My Multia came with it.

> Mine has a SCSI controller (weak little card, actually) with a 50 pin
> connector and a drive tray that takes up the expansion slot. What else can
> be used here? Video....NIC...? I would imagine there is a special adapter
> for these other things?

There's a riser card that lets you put one PCI card in that area.  My
Multia also has the 50-pin SCSI riser and mount for a 3.5" drive (much
cheaper and easier to find).  With the PCI riser, you're stuck with a
2.5" IDE drive (slow), a 2.5" SCSI drive (expensive), or an external
SCSI drive (cumbersome).

> There is also the issue of the connectors behind the "cage" in the front of
> the board. It seems that there is an IDE-like connector, and an orifice for
> a ribbon.  What are these for?

There *is* an IDE connector there.  I've not used it, though.  I've
got a 3.5" SCSI drive in mine.

> That is about it. I guess the relevancy of these questions are that I wish
> to install Linux on this box. Judging by the sounds of some stories other
> users are telling, it ought to be a bumpy ride.

I found the easiest way to install Linux on my Multia was to boot it
up via floppy and do an FTP install from another machine on my home
network.

