Well, luckily, you can get the Compaq (formerly Digital)
compilers (both C and fortran) from Compaq as part of their
Linux beta program.  Speedups of more than 100% are easily
possible over programs compiled with egcs, the Linux
native compiler.  You can access the signup page at:

 http://www.compaq.com/linux

Speaking of 'try before you buy' Compaq also has a test
drive program where you can log into their latest servers
running Linux, Tru64, *BSD.  Check it out at:

 http://www.testdrive.compaq.com/

Compaq may eventually decide to charge money for the compilers
we have no idea what they may charge, but the beta's work
pretty well.  You can't compile the kernel with them yet.

Oh yea, they also have their accelerated math libs as well
as their 'extended' math lib, which contains FFT, BLAS, and
a whole bunch of other things.

If you have any other questions, Compaq is available at
fortran@compaq.com   Support is available through:

 http://www.iol.unh.edu/linux

Hope you have fun with all this, it takes about 100MB to
install it all, but it's well worth it.

