KQH <keithNOkeSPAM@betor.dialix.oz.au.invalid> wrote in message
news:057a90f4.4fe30c05@usw-ex0102-009.remarq.com...
> I have used fdisk, cfdisk and Yast, variously, to partition and
> format a Jaz 2Gb drive. The formating appears to terminate correctly
> and subsequent uses of fdisk recognise the partitions. However when
> I try to mount either the drive or any partition of it I get a
> complaint about not being able to recognise the filesystem or that
> the superblock is mangled.
...

Those programs you tried don't format a disk, they only
partition it and designate the partitions for a certain filesystem. You
must subsequently use mke2fs to put a filesystem on the partitions
you define with fdisk, cfdisk and Yast (this is the formatting). The
errors you describe are typical of an uninitialised filesystem. The
one time you succeeded in mounting a partition was apparently
accidental; the (uninitialised) superblock looked just enough like
a valid one to fool mount.
Good luck!

Martin Sinot
