Eberhard Burr (Eberhard.Burr@gmx.de) wrote:
: rraffer1@osf1.gmu.edu (Ryan M Rafferty) writes:

: > Faster compiler generally implies a faster kernel, though of course
: > that simplistic relationship will not hold true in all cases.

: I would think that a system which spends substantial amounts of time
: in kernelland is seriously flawed in it's hardware. The most I can get 
: is somewhere fairly below 10% CPU time in kernel but then all data
: paths are saturated anyway. (remote X saturating the network, full
: speed download over the modem and find saturating the disk...) I
: suspect I wouldn't realize the difference if it was at only 2% CPU in
: kernel.

Of course. But an improved kernel would certainly benefit old, slow
systems that spend a lot of time doing a lot of varied tasks, such
as my old noname-based server that handles all the menial tasks on
the network, from ip-masq and dial-up to printserving (using printer
filters + ghostscript), NFS, SAMBA sharing, and even running Bnetd!

Honestly, even with these tasks running simultaneously the system
appears to have pep to spare. But if I decide to bog the machine further
with more tasks, I would appreciate speedier code in the kernel
"just in case." Of course, you might well argue that I should stop
being so cheap and just upgrade the workhorse :).

: don't mistake a faster kernel for a faster computer. The scheduler and 
: some parts of the VM layer could win, of course.

I'm not mistaking anything. But depending on the tasks of a particular
machine, further kernel optimizations may or may not enhance overall
performance. The key is to "optimize the inner loop", as it were.

I do believe that compiling GNU LibC would likely yield more overall
benefit than the kernel, however. It would be interesting to attempt
that and run an empirical benchmark or two.

Ryan

